  <info><![CDATA[## WHAT IS IT?

Scenario 1+E (S1+E) of the agent-based model for the paper "Capital Conversion, Debt and Interconnectivity: A Formal Approach to Inequality among Nomadic Herders in Ancient Eurasia". Households in need receive animals from a wealthy patron as a gift, and goods from outside the steppe replace the animals they lose in disasters. Tests H4 (interconnectivity), compared with Scenario 1; reference for Scenario 3+E.

## HOW IT WORKS

100 households start with 60 animals each. Every year (one tick), with the default values, the operations below take place in this order, as in Appendix 2 of the paper; within each operation, households act one at a time in random order:

- External goods (`receive-external-goods`): the yearly inflow, `inflow-level` of the carrying capacity in head of livestock, is divided equally among the recipients of the `channel`: the richest 10% of the year by total wealth (`"top"`), the households still present among the quarter drawn at random at the start of the run (`"frontier"`) or all households (`"diffuse"`). The goods are staples, usable only in the year they arrive.
- Growth (`grow-herds`): herds grow by 10% while the total livestock does not exceed the carrying capacity; households grow one at a time, in random order, as in Shultz and Costopoulos (2019).
- Disasters (`disasters`): individual disasters (10% per year for each household) and general disasters (10% per year, striking each household with a probability of 40%) halve the herds they strike; a household struck by both loses three quarters. Each household's goods replace its own animals just lost, not those it holds on loan.
- Loss of status (`check-patrons`): a patron whose herd falls below 500 loses his status and all his clients.
- Help (`seek-help`): a household below 60 animals asks its patron, if it has one, for the animals it needs to get back to 60. If the patron cannot give them and still keep 500, or if the household has no patron, it looks for one, chosen at random among the households that can. A client that finds one leaves its old patron; one that finds none stays with him, as in Shultz and Costopoulos. No household can become the patron of its own patron. A client can itself become a patron, as in Shultz and Costopoulos.
- Dissolution (`dissolve`): a household below 2 animals that no patron can help dissolves and is removed from the simulation.
- Measures (`update-measures`): the measures of the year are recorded.

At the end of every year the code checks its own bookkeeping and stops with an error if something is inconsistent.

## HOW TO USE IT

**Press `Setup` and then `Run`. Results will appear in the monitor widgets progressively while the simulation is running.**

The parameters are global variables of the Code tab, with their default values in `set-parameters`. To change one, type for example `set carrying-capacity 50000` before `setup`, or vary it in BehaviorSpace; `set-defaults` restores all the default values. A parameter equal to 0 is treated as not set: to use 0, set it after `setup` (for example `setup set general-disaster-rate 0`).

The experiments of the paper are saved in BehaviorSpace (Tools > BehaviorSpace), with every parameter set explicitly and 100 repetitions for each combination of values; the seed of each run is recorded in `run-seed`. The run measures exclude the first 300 years (`warm-up`), so that they do not depend on the initial conditions. The metrics are recorded every 100 years and at the end of each run. For speed, untick "Update view" and "Update plots and monitors" when you start an experiment, and choose the table output.

## THINGS TO NOTICE

Yearly values: `households`, `livestock`, `gini-wealth` (Gini of total wealth: animals owned, including those lent out), `gini-herds` (Gini of the herds kept), `number-of-networks`, `largest-network`, `mean-network-size`, `number-of-clients`, `mean-patron-herd`, `mean-patron-wealth`, `patron-gap-herd` and `patron-gap-wealth` (richest minus poorest patron) and `patron-records` (each patron: who, direct herd, total wealth, clients).

Run values: `mean-gini-wealth`, `mean-gini-herds`, `gini-first-500`, `gini-last-500`, `gini-peak` with `gini-peak-year`, `half-gone-year` (-1 if half of the households never disappeared), `dissolved-households`, `persistence-25-wealth` (share of the richest 10% still among them after 25 years), `persistence-200-wealth` (share that remain among them without interruption for 200 years), the same two by herd kept (`persistence-25-herds`, `persistence-200-herds`), `disaster-loss-share`, `mean-number-of-networks`, `mean-largest-network`, `mean-network-size-run`, `networks-observed`, `mean-network-duration` (networks still active at the end counted with the years observed; their share is `share-of-open-networks`), `mean-contract-length`, `returns-to-dependence`, `patron-crisis-survival`, `patron-herd-change`, `patron-wealth-change`, `recipient-loss-share` and `other-loss-share`. A run value that is not defined in a run, for example the mean network duration when no network was observed, is reported as NA; the yearly network values are 0 in a year without patrons.

## CREDITS AND REFERENCES

Based on Shultz, Daniel R., and Andre Costopoulos. 2019. "Modeling Environmental Variability and Network Formation among Pastoral Nomadic Households: Implications for the Rise of the Mongol Empire." PLOS ONE 14 (10): e0223677. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0223677

Code repository: https://github.com/ArcheoHacker1501/ABM_Nomadic_Herders_Inequality
]]></info>
